      "text": "Imagine a language model as a giant, silent librarian. When you ask it to write a sentence, it doesn't just read your words; it acts like a detective, scanning the entire library to find the perfect book. It starts by reading the **prompt** (your question), which acts as the master key. Then, it looks at the **previous tokens** (words it has already written) to see if they fit the story. If the previous words create a confusing sentence, it might backtrack and try a different word.\n\nThe process is a continuous loop: it reads the prompt, checks the history, and generates a new token. This happens one by one, like a robot typing letter by letter. However, if you enable **sampling**, the model doesn't stop after one word. Instead, it keeps reading the prompt and history, trying to find the next best fit. This means the same model can generate different answers because it is constantly exploring the vast possibilities of the text, much like a human guessing a word in a long sentence.",
